ueness is not guaranteed","Exactly one such interior point $c$ must exist","Both endpoint derivatives $f'(a)$ and $f'(b)$ are zero","No stationary point exists inside the interval"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2c5b726e9bed6da70bf631d8","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_rolles_theorem_82b49c14_006","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"How is Rolle’s theorem related to the mean value theorem?","options":["The mean value theorem is unrelated to Rolle’s theorem.","Rolle’s theorem excludes differentiability.","Rolle’s theorem is a special case of the mean value theorem.","Rolle’s theorem applies only to sequences."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"721e4859baa781fe66bff163","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_rolles_theorem_82b49c14_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f$ satisfy the hypotheses of Rolle’s theorem on $[2,5]$. Where is the guaranteed point $c$ located?","options":["$c = 2$ only","$c = 5$ only","$c\\notin[2,5]$","$c\\in(2,5)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0130ea4a9f2b3b31e1e556b4","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_rolles_theorem_82b49c14_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For $f(x) = \\sin x$ on $[0,\\pi]$, which point satisfies the Rolle conclusion?","options":["$c = 0$","$c = \\pi$","$c = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$","$c = 2\\pi$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ad86d9307218331d2b33b9d5","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_rolles_theorem_82b49c14_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which missing hypothesis can invalidate an attempted use of Rolle’s theorem?","options":["Differentiability on $(a,b)$","The existence of a set","The notation $\\mathbb{Q}$","Having exactly one representation as a fraction"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f566504f1778c1f0224c00d4","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_rolles_theorem_82b49c14_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why must the condition $f(a) = f(b)$ be checked before applying Rolle’s theorem?","options":["It is one of the theorem’s hypotheses.","It is the theorem’s conclusion.","It is only needed for sequences.","It ensures $a = b$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3a466fb7d59250c8ac00964d","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"no_diagnostic_value"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_rolles_theorem_82b49c14_011","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"A differentiable function is continuous on $[a,b]$ and has $f(a) \\neq f(b)$. What can be concluded from Rolle’s theorem alone?","options":["Rolle still guarantees an interior zero derivative","The function must attain an interior maximum","Rolle does not apply because the endpoint values differ","The interval must therefore be unbounded"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"44626e3fd1ac354db092cece","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_rolles_theorem_82b49c14_012","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"A function satisfies Rolle’s theorem on $[a,b]$ and has two